data: {"id":"chatcmpl-20261018174744276627818c955d568qp6J4iqz","created":1792345664,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_e9870d837c7e4474830b234f","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":"{\"content\":\"# Proof Obligations: `add_one`\\n\\nProperty under proof (from `arith.spec.md`):\\n\\n```\\n∀ x ∈ ℤ :  add_one(x) == x + 1\\n```\\n\\nTo establish this property as a theorem about the implementation in\\n`arith.py`, the following obligations must be discharged.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## OB1 — Well-formedness (signature)\\n\\n**Statement.** `add_one` is a unary function accepting one positional\\nargument, and its defining expression `x + 1` is well-typed for the declared\\ndomain.\\n\\n**Discharge.** By inspection of `arith.py`: the definition is\\n`def add_one(x): return x + 1`, which is a unary function. In Python, `+`\\nbetween any object and the `int` literal `1` resolves via `x.__add__(1)`\\nand, on `TypeError`, via reflected `(1).__radd__(x)`; `int.__radd__`\\nrestricts operands to `int` instances, so for `x` an `int` the expression\\nalways evaluates to an `int`.\\n\\n**Status:** discharged by inspection.\\n\\n## OB2 — Domain coverage (the quantifier is over ℤ, not the tested sample)\\n\\n**Statement.** The universal quantifier ranges over *all* integers, an\\ninfinite set. The check in `verify_arith.py` (`python verify_arith.py`)\\ncovers 2014 sample values and therefore does not by itself establish the\\nproperty.\\n\\n**Discharge options (any one):**\\n- (a) A mechanized proof in a proof assistant (Lean, Coq/ Rocq, Isabelle,\\n  Dafny) after re-expressing the function in that system.\\n- (b) A closed-form argument (see OB3) that is itself formally validated.\\n- (c) SMT encoding: model `add_one` as `λx. x + 1` and assert\\n  `∀x. add_one(x) = x + 1` over the SMT `Int` sort; discharged by the\\n  theory of linear integer arithmetic (Z3/CVC5: `forall x. f(x) == x + 1`).\\n\\n**Status:** open. Any \\\"proof\\\" resting solely on `verify_arith.py` fails\\nthis obligation.\\n\\n## OB3 — Semantics agreement (implementation = specification)\\n\\n**Statement.** The Python expression `x + 1`, for `x` bound to an `int`,\\ncomputes the mathematical successor `succ(x)` — i.e., the CPython semantics\\nof `+` on arbitrary-precision `int` coincides with the Peano successor\\noperation modulo the binary representation.\\n\\n**Sub-obligations.**\\n- **OB3.1 — Correctness:** for `x ∈ ℤ`, `x + 1` evaluates to\\n  `succ(x)` as a value of type `int`.\\n- **OB3.2 — Absence of overflow:** the result stays in-range. Discharged by\\n  CPython's `int` being arbitrary precision (no fixed-width wraparound), so\\n  `OB3.2` holds trivially. (Contrast: NumPy `int64` or C `int` would make\\n  this obligation substantive — `INT_MAX + 1` wraps and the property would\\n  be false on finite-width types.)\\n- **OB3.3 — No subclass interference:** an `int` subclass overriding\\n  `__add__` would break the property even though `isinstance(x, int)` holds\\n  (demonstrated: a `Bad(int)` with `__add__ → 0` returns `0`, not `6`). Two\\n  defences, either of which discharges the obligation:\\n  - restrict the domain to exactly `type(x) is int`, or\\n  - appeal to `int` being an immutable type: its arithmetic slots cannot be\\n    monkeypatched at runtime (`type 'int'` rejects attribute assignment).\\n\\n**Status:** OB3.1 discharged by CPython semantics; OB3.2 discharged\\n(arbitrary precision); OB3.3 discharged under the \\\"exactly `int`\\\" domain\\nrestriction.\\n\\n## OB4 — Purity / referential transparency\\n\\n**Statement.** `add_one` has no observable side effects: it performs no I/O,\\nmutates no state, raises no exception on the declared domain, and always\\nterminates.\\n\\n**Discharge.** The body is a single pure expression on a local parameter.\\nTermination: one constant-time arithmetic operation. `bool` inputs\\n(`bool ⊂ int`) flow through the inherited `int.__add__` and return `int`\\n(`True + 1 == 2`), so no exception arises on any `int`-domain input.\\n\\n**Status:** discharged by inspection.\\n\\n## OB5 — Equivalence with the tested property (verification soundness)\\n\\n**Statement.** The runtime check in `verify_arith.py` tests the same\\nrelation the spec states — no transcription drift between the formal\\nproperty, the spec prose, and the checked assertion — and the checker itself\\nis sound (asserts, no exception swallowing).\\n\\n**Discharge.** By inspection: the checker asserts `add_one(x) == x + 1`\\nverbatim, over `INTERESTING` (0, ±1, 8/16/32/64-bit boundaries, 10^30\\narbitrary-precision values) plus a dense sweep `range(-1000, 1001)`, and\\nfails loudly via `assert` on any counterexample.\\n\\n**Status:** discharged by inspection (soundness of the check, not coverage —\\ncoverage is OB2's concern).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n| ID    | Obligation                        | Status                          |\\n|-------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------|\\n| OB1   | Well-formedness                   | discharged (inspection)         |\\n| OB2   | Domain coverage / true proof      | **open** (needs OB3-based proof, proof assistant, or SMT) |\\n| OB3.1 | Semantics: `+` = successor on ℤ   | discharged (CPython semantics)  |\\n| OB3.2 | No overflow                       | discharged (arbitrary precision)|\\n| OB3.3 | No subclass interference          | discharged (domain: exactly `int`) |\\n| OB4   | Purity, termination               | discharged (inspection)         |\\n| OB5   | Checker tests the stated property | discharged (inspection)         |\\n\\nThe property is established modulo OB2, which requires a real proof artifact\\n(proof assistant or SMT certificate); testing alone cannot close it.\\n\",\"path\":\"arith.obligations.md\"}"}}]}}]}

